selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The half-moon manifold section: a chamber wall on one side, an ellipse on the other.</p><p>A manifold wrapped around a thrust chamber is not a free shape. One side of it\nis the chamber itself \u2013 whatever the wall happens to do at that station \u2013 and\nthe other is a dome the designer picks. So the section is an arbitrary inner\ncurve, a half-ellipse outboard of it, and a fillet at each end blending the two.\nReal hardware of this kind reads as a D at the cylindrical part of a chamber and\nas a kidney where the wall curves.</p>", "a[href=\"#how-it-is-placed\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">How it is placed<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#how-it-is-placed\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>An anchor station fixes the slit, and it stays there at every azimuth \u2013\nchannels come out where they come out. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">slit_placement</span></code> says where the slit\nsits about that anchor, and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">exposure_placement</span></code> says how the wall left over\nonce the slit is accounted for is divided either side of it. That division is\nheld at every azimuth too, so as the area law closes the section down the\nexposed wall shrinks onto the slit from both sides in a fixed ratio, and reaches\nit exactly at <code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">minimum_size</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#measured-along-the-wall\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Measured along the wall<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#measured-along-the-wall\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The slit and the width are arc lengths along the wall, not axial extents. The\nslit is space reserved on the wall surface for cooling channels to break out\ninto, and on a sloped wall the surface runs further than the station span does:\na nozzle at 28 degrees gives only 12.4 mm of wall for every 14 mm of <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">x</span></code>.\nReserving it axially would quietly hand back a tenth of the room the channels\nneed. On a cylindrical wall the two measures coincide.</p><p>The slit is a reservation, not modelled geometry. Where the channels actually\nbreak through, how they are divided and what the fairing looks like is CAD\nwork; what pyskyfire draws is the cavity they open into, with enough of the wall\nkept clear that the breakout has somewhere to go.</p>", "a[href=\"#how-it-is-sized\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">How it is sized<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#how-it-is-sized\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The same three regimes a volute works through, for the same reason. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">size</span></code> is\nthe exposed arc of wall, and shrinking it is the first and cheapest way to take\narea out. Once it has closed onto the slit there is no wall left to give up, so\nthe ellipse\u2019s aspect ratio takes over and flattens the dome instead. When that\nreaches <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">max_aspect_ratio</span></code> the family has run out of knobs, and the area law\ngets clipped to the smallest section it can build.</p>", "a[href=\"InnerProfile.html#pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.InnerProfile\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.InnerProfile\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">InnerProfile</span></span></dt><dd><p>A wall curve in the meridional plane, measured by arc length.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Build one with <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../regen/outer_wall/OuterWall.html#pyskyfire.regen.outer_wall.OuterWall.profile\" title=\"pyskyfire.regen.outer_wall.OuterWall.profile\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">profile()</span></code></a> to take\nthe wall off a resolved thrust chamber, or directly to state a wall\npyskyfire did not resolve.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"HalfMoonSection.html#pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.HalfMoonSection\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.HalfMoonSection\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">HalfMoonSection</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">profile</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">slit_height</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">fillet_radius</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">slit_placement</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.5</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exposure_placement</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.5</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">aspect_ratio</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">1.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">max_aspect_ratio</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../volute_section/VoluteSection.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.VoluteSection\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.VoluteSection\"><code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.VoluteSection</span></code></a></p><p>A manifold section: chamber wall inboard, half-ellipse outboard.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#closing-the-ends\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Closing the ends<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#closing-the-ends\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The fillets are circular arcs of the radius given \u2013 a real corner radius, the\nkind a print or a cutter leaves \u2013 tangent to the wall at each end of the\nexposed arc and tangent to the ellipse. Where they touch the ellipse is solved\nrather than assumed, because the offset curve of an ellipse is not an ellipse.\nThe ellipse is carried on the chord between the two ends of the exposed wall and\nis centred on it, which is what closes the construction: the chord fixes the\nframe, one tangency solve fixes the ellipse, and both fillets follow from it.</p><p>Working on the chord means the fillets are tangent to the chord rather than to\nthe wall\u2019s own slope at the point they meet it. The two differ by how far the\nwall turns across half a section, which for a manifold on a chamber is a couple\nof degrees at most, and the inner boundary still follows the wall exactly.</p>", "a[href=\"#classes\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Classes<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#classes\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
